 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victorian women's job market has softened over the past year, but it has steadied in recent months. The figures below are ABS Labour Force data, seasonally adjusted, for September 2026.\n\n**Women in Victoria, September 2026**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%. It was 4.1% in September 2025 and was below 4.1% in some months of late 2025. It rose through early 2026 and peaked at 5.2% in July, so it is up about 1 percentage point over the year.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% a year earlier. It dipped to 62.4% in April 2026 and has partly recovered.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from 61.3% a year earlier. The low was 59.5% in April.\n- **Employed women:** about 1.843 million, roughly flat on a year ago (1.838 million). Employment fell to about 1.803 million in April and has since recovered.\n- **Full-time employment:** about 1.008 million, about the same as a year earlier (1.011 million).\n\n**Compared with men in Victoria**\n- Men's unemployment rate was 5.0% in September 2026, down from 5.3% a year earlier.\n- Women's unemployment rate was 4.1% in September 2025, below men's, and is now slightly above them at 5.1%.\n- Men's participation rate is 71.5%, down from 72.3% a year earlier.\n\n**Summary:** Employment numbers have held up, with no net job losses over the year. The population has grown faster than the number of jobs, so the unemployment rate is higher and the share of women in work is lower. The worst of the deterioration was around April to July 2026. Since then the unemployment rate has eased slightly (5.2% to 5.1%) and employment has recovered. Monthly state figures for one group are noisy, so I wouldn't read much into a single month's move.",
